t knows from  the get go that  this code represents just the letter e  or the at sign or something like that it  doesn't need to take any space for those  in the table  so it's able to do a bit more patterns  in the same amount of space that way  which means it can compress more  effectively  and another reason that lzw is a good  example  is it was at the heart of a really nasty  patent situation  if any of you remember the burn all  gifts thing  the the gif image format has lzw  designed into it as part of the standard  but lcw was actually patented at the  time  and you had to get a license to use it  which  conflicted really badly with how it was  becoming a very ubiquitous image file  format  so among other things the the ping image  file format came out of trying to design  one that  didn't have those sort of ip  restrictions  and then that patent has been expired  for a while but there's a lot of other  compression methods that  are patented and i'm just not even going  to bring them up  because you really can't do anything  with them  and another one is deflate which  is used in gzip and it was also used in  pkzip  on windows and i believe it's used in  the standard zip file format for  compression on windows  and so if you uh if you use the z-lib  library to do data compression and you  don't  specify a particular algorithm it'll  default to this it'll default to having  like a 64 kilobyte hash table  in memory that it uses with character  trigrams  three consecutive characters to do  dispatch to try to find patterns faster  and it has a special block header that  says for this next data passing through  do we want to use helpmancoding  do we want to use lcss or do we want to  use both of them in combination one than  the other  or some content if it's already been  compressed like if it's  video data that's been encoded some way  or something then it can also just  pass that data through without doing  anything to it  because if you take a bunch of data and  then you have to put  one bit marker on every single byte  saying oh literally this literally this  literally this  then it just expands it by 1 8 and  there's no point in doing that  and then finally for the lossless  compression i'm going to talk about the  boro's wheeler transform  but so besides trying to search for  repeated patterns  you can also pre-process data to make it  sort better  or to make it compress better  and a particularly obvious  transformation is  if you sorted data it would be a lot  easier to compress well because you'd  have long runs of the same characters  and uh there's a guy who made a version  of the king james bible that has been  fully  sorted letter by letter  and so there's just can you see that  okay okay cool  and so there's just pages and pages of  ease and stuff and obviously  that would compress a lot better than if  you were trying to work with that with  all the words in their normal order  but the problem with sorting like that  is that it's a one-way process  you can't sort something and then  compress it and then magically put  everything  else back in the original order now if  only there was  one weird trick you could do to undo the  sword  now there's this transformation called  the burrows wheeler transform  which is sort of partially sorting but  in a particular  unusual way that you're able to undo and  this has the effect  that if you have a lot of very localized  patterns like  th in english is a good one you have the  you have there you have this  that stuff like that it will tend to  group together  so that you'll get a lot of runs of ts  and h's adjacent to each other and so  the the  output from that transformation will  tend to compress more effectively  and um if you've ever used i believe  it's actually called bzip2  now the b there stands for the burroughs  wheeler transform  that's what distinguishes that and  that's why it's often more effective at  compressing  certain kinds of data so the way that  this works  um usually this is done in large blocks  but i'm  trying to stick with what you can  actually see on a slide so  i just picked the word repetition here  now if you add a starting marker to this  and then take all of the single  character rotations of that  so you see there's repetition and then  you drop the first character and put it  at the end of the line  and each line is is slided slid over  by one bite now if you do that  and then you sort those lines  lexicographically  so now uh the start one is still at the  beginning but all of the other lines  have been rearranged  so you see it goes e e i i n  and then t is at the end because  alphabetically that's the last line to  appear there  and if you just take that last column  even in this small example you can see  that two of the t's wound up together  because repetition is repetitive  now you can take that last column of  that matrix  and compress that but you can also undo  that transformation and the way that you  do that is  if you if you save that last column and  then sort it again  and then take that column and tack that  onto the front of it  in the next to last column and then  sort that and then add the column again  to it  and then just keep ticking through that  you can see the original string  start to emerge there  and then you sort it one last time and  because there's a special start marker  on it you know which of those rotations  is the original string  and there we go  and the other funny thing with this is  the people who discovered this  had no idea what to do with it  they're just like wow it's amazing that  this works and it never fails to amaze  me  that this works um but it was over  10 years before they realized oh this  would actually be really useful for data  compression  and until then i don't know if they just  like  impressed people with it at parties or  something like that  um but also uh actual implementations of  this  don't use like a big matrix of  characters they use one array with a  bunch of offsets in it  but conceptually it's a lot easier to  think of it as working that way  but since it might be running like a 64  kilobyte block or something you wouldn't  want to square the size of that in  memory because that would  take up a dreadful amount of space and  speaking of memory so recently i was  working on an embedded project  and i needed data compression for that  and uh before i get into that how many  of you have done any embedded work or  like  cool or arduinos stuff like that  okay uh keep your hands up how many of  you have ever run gzip on an arduino  yeah exactly gzip assumes a minimum of  64k of memory for its hash table and  that's just  completely out of the question and a lot  of embedded projects you might have  you know a few kilobytes of ram maybe  512 bytes  depending on what kind of chip you're on  and also a lot of embedded projects are  hard real-time systems  so you might not have an os at all or  you may have  a real-time os which is just barely a  scheduler and a couple ipc  primitives and so if you have a lot of  data you don't want to  run through compressing or decompressing  that all in one blocking step  because at that point either the car has  exploded or  the usb bus has decided that you're not  actually connected anymore because  you're not responding to the keep alive  packet once every millisecond  or that sort of thing or if you're like  parsing radio data as it's streaming in  you'll completely fall out of sync with  it  so i looked around for options for data  compression that would actually work in  an embedded system  and came up empty-handed there were a  couple  decompression in particular that were  available but some of them had bad  licenses  some of them used more space than i  actually was able to use  most of them worked in one pass and i  didn't really see anything that did  compression on an embedded system  which is important because you often  have like  very repetitive sensor data that you're  reading periodically  and you mostly care about when it  changes but you want to record  everything that you can  and then do processing on it later and  if you can collapse a lot of very  similar data together  you can do a lot more with the tiny tiny  bit of space that you have  so i made an implementation of lzss  which i called heat shrink  which is the the sliding window  technique  and this is designed so that it scales  way way way down and it can do real  useful data decompression in under 50  bytes of memory  and if you want to do compression on the  embedded system it takes about 100 bytes  if you have more memory than that it can  take advantage of it and compress more  effectively  but that's that's the bare minimum to  get something useful out of it  and and most systems can at least manage  that and it doesn't take a whole lot of  rom space either  the code is usually under a kilobyte  when it's compiled  and that's under a bsd style license  i actually had to find a particular bsd  variant license  that didn't have weird considerations  for when stuff was distributed only as  hardware which is  something that i hadn't really thought  of evaluating licenses but turns out to  be really important  so the way that i implement oh and i  chose lzss  because the output buffer is also the  working set  for the decompression patterns which  means that it can get by with a much  smaller amount of space  than uh other things that need temporary  data structures for all  for like a huffman tree or something  like that while they're working  so the way that i implemented this was  by  creating a big formal state machine for  it  where i was able to represent all the  transitions and ensure that if  i had to suspend and resume on  individual bits of i o  because this is being co-routine with  other stuff that's going on  then i could be sure to keep the  information that i needed and then be  able to restore that later on to  continue where i left off  and so the api for this uh my favorite  way of describing it is  something where you dump in data and  then you turn the crank and then you  just keep turning the crank until  nothing more comes out  and then you dump in more data and keep  turning and so on and so on  and once you're done you tell it that  you aren't that you aren't going to have  any more input  and then keep turning the crank until  you reach the end of the output stream  and then tear the whole thing down  and modeling this as a state machine  helped shake out a lot of edge cases  so the the blue transitions here are  where the user of the library  is inputting data into it and the red  transitions are where it is  sending output into an output buffer  that the user is supplied  and then there are also many states that  actually have red  and blue arcs on them  and that way if if it's expanding a very  very large pattern  it can expand it step by step even if  there's a very small buffer that that's  going into it'll fill it up  say that it's done you copy that  somewhere else or do whatever with it  and then after that you  call it again and then it fills up the  buffer again and you just incrementally  step through all of the output  and um also by controlling the size of  these buffers  you can also configure how much work it  does in a single step  so if you have very very hard real-time  guarantees and you have to do all of  your processing and say  200 microseconds or something per step  that's that's an option  and i chose the decompression state  machine for this because  most of the complexity is in the  compression and that will not fit on one  slide  but the other thing i like about this  diagram is that it actually has  a bug in it and it's the first thing  that jumps out at me now  but um when i originally made the  library the the size of the offset  into the sliding window was always eight  bits or less  so that was one byte that i could buffer  inside the data structure for it  accumulate the bits for it and then when  it was done move on  and just before i released the library i  increased that  because sometimes it's useful to have  say  a four kilobyte buffer or something and  that's what 12 bits  so that way you might be reading like a  length counter out of  the bit stream but then that means that  there's actually more than one bite  involved there  and i didn't want to increase the size  of the accumulator because of some  alignment issues and stuff and it would  have wound up taking a lot more space  all just like ugly c details but so  uh what i had to do was take this state  here  where if it was accumulating 12 bits  from  the input stream and it got five of them  and then suspended and then resumed it  would throw those away  um it wouldn't retain them properly so i  had to take that step and split it in  half  to getting the upper couple bits if if  the size was defined to be that large  and then accumulate the lower bits  and then that worked fine so  here's a demo of heat shrink running on  a big block of text  this is a software license agreement and  like all software license agreements it  says that you should not use it to  design manufacture or produce nuclear  missiles  chemical or biological weapons  any of you want to guess what this is  the license for  i'm sorry yeah itunes good guess  so i passed this through heat shrink  and it goes from that to that  and each one of those pound signs there  is 11 bits  rather than an 8 bit character this is  assuming an ascii  encoding for that and i specifically  chose  a software license because if you look  at some of the patterns that it picks up  on  it's very clear that this is in legalese  this this can be like a nice  way of fingerprinting a document and  seeing what the significant phrases are  in it  even if you don't actually care about  reducing the size of it and that's a  pretty cheap operation  so it noticed that the big cumbersome  phrase  the licensed application if it could  represent that in a couple bits  there was a really major size savings  and likewise united states law  without limitation though and may not be  and  in the case of if out limitation the  it's dropping the leading w  i assume because sometimes it's  uppercase sometimes it's lowercase  um but it also picked up  on some kind of counter-intuitive  substitutions  like chun actional and edini  but when lzs has a really really big  buffer to dip into it can just  pick up on a lot of stuff like that so  also there's lossy compression which is  a lot more data specific  lossless compression has to work in the  general case but lossy compression  assumes that you're working with a  certain kind of image data or audio data  or something like that  and it's it's really a kind of smart  degradation of that content where it can  throw away details that the human eye  might not pick up on or  most people won't be able to hear and  there's like a slider there  for if you don't mind if the quality of  it starts to get low but you need it to  be really really small you can start  cutting a little deeper  and so i have an example here where i  have this  image three times on the left there's  the original image  in the middle i've decreased the quality  of it so it's actually half the file  size it goes from 51k to 27k  but you can't really tell a difference  unless you look at it really close and  some details like the edges or the  reflections in the glass or the shadows  or the wood grain  tend to show that more but then i  have the size of that one more time and  it goes from 27 to 14k  and then that gets really pixely because  the way that  jpeg compression works um the the  primary way that it works is breaking an  image  into an 8x8 pixel grid and getting the  average color  and then applying a bunch of a certain  number of  gradients over that that then add other  details onto it and they're translucent  and the way that those overlap  ends up defining most of the detail in  the image  so in closing i've talked about why data  compression matters  how to design uh data so that it can be  compressed more effectively  uh talked about lossless compression  algorithms and then there's the case  study of heat shrink  and the example lossy compression  so if you want to learn more about this  here are two books that i particularly  like  managing gigabytes and information  theory inference and learning algorithms  the letter which also has a bunch of  machine learning and some other topics  in it  and there's certainly overlap there and  there's a free pdf of that on the  author's site if you're interested  and also i work for a really cool  transparent company in michigan  and if you want to know if you know  anyone who wants to do cool work for a  small company we're hiring at our  detroit and our offices  so i have a couple minutes for questions  i think  anybody yes  um  okay uh to to try to summarize that for  the people who can't hear you  uh he he he said that if you you  if you're doing uh compression on a  known data type you can pre-compute but  a good table of substitutions would be  and then build that into your  application and not need to represent  that explicitly in the data stream  and and that's a case where you know a  lot more about your problem domain  than just a very very general  compression algorithm  and there's a certain point where that  turns into designing a binary  coding for your data format by hand but  um  it's it's good to know that tools like  zlib can also automate a lot of that  work  uh anybody else in the back  that that what i don't know  i okay okay yeah and and i i couldn't  fit lcma into this talk i kind of type  for time as is so  yeah there's there's a bunch of other  algorithms i mostly focused on those  particular ones because of the ones that  i'm most familiar with but i was also  looking at ones that tended to scale  down  rather than up there's some some  intere